# Structure vs Search

Research project comparing agent-controlled retrieval with graph-based
retrieval for multi-hop question answering over document collections.

## Research question

When is graph structure worth its cost for multi-hop document QA, and can
lexical connectivity predict when agent-controlled sparse retrieval is sufficient?

## Planned experimental systems

- A0: One-shot BM25 retrieval with a shared reader model.
- A1: Iterative agentic BM25 retrieval.
- A2: Document-aware sparse retrieval controls, if justified by the corpus audit.
- D: Agentic dense retrieval.
- S: HippoRAG 2 graph-based retrieval.
- R: A router that starts with sparse retrieval and escalates to graph retrieval
  when its test-time evidence signals justify doing so.

These are planned experiments, not completed results.

## Evaluation

The project will track answer quality, evidence usage, model input/output tokens,
model calls, latency, and graph construction cost. Graph build cost will be
reported separately and included in amortized per-query cost estimates.

## Experimental safeguards

- Keep the reader model and answer-generation settings consistent across systems
  wherever feasible.
- Match evidence-token budgets and log actual inference cost separately.
- Tune routing thresholds on development data, not the test set.
- Do not use gold supporting passages or gold answers as test-time routing inputs.
- Do not commit raw datasets, indexes, model weights, credentials, or run outputs.

## Project status

Initial repository scaffold. Dataset loading, retrieval implementations,
and experiments are not yet complete.

## Repository layout

- `configs/`: dataset, model, budget, and experiment configuration.
- `data/`: local datasets and generated data; large files are excluded from Git.
- `src/structure_vs_search/`: research implementation.
- `scripts/`: experiment entry points and corpus audit.
- `tests/`: automated tests.
- `docs/`: research and evaluation protocol.
